  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1244 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nRest of today through late next week (Thursday)...\n\nRest of today through Saturday: GOES East water vapor and RAP \nanalysis indicate mean trough off of OR and WA coastlines, mean \nridge across the Plains and deep mean longwave trough over the Great \nLakes to OH Valley. This is keeping northwesterly dry flow and \nushering in much drier (PWs around half to quarter inch) and much \ncolder air into the region. Surface analysis indicates 1028mb \nsurface high centered over south-central Canada, with 1024mb high \npressure isobars extending well southwestward into the central \nPlains to near the ArkLaTex to Gulf Coast region. Dry and cold \nweather will be the norm through the overnight hours as the \nnorthwesterly low-level trajectories keep cold conditions around. \nThis will lead to a likely freeze overnight, with persistent blended \nfreeze probs (40 to 70 percent as far south as Interstate 20 and \ngreater than 70 to 90 percent along and east of Interstate 55 and \nnorth of Interstate 20). There are some probs that extend westward \nalong the MS River corridor up into northeast LA and southeast AR. \nBased on that and some convective allowing models near 33F to 35F\nrange, even if probs are a touch lower (25 to 40 percent), there \nis enough support of lows at or less than 35F to support freeze \npotential. Expanded inherited Freeze Warning westward along \nInterstate 20 to include portions of western MS along the MS River\ncorridor, northeast LA and north into extreme southeast AR. \nAdjusted the HWO freeze graphic accordingly. Highs and lows will \nbe seasonably cool in this regime, some 6F to 14F below (highs \ntoday and Saturday of 55F to 65F and lows tonight 28F to 34F north\nof Interstate 20 and 35F to 38F to the south and similar but more\nconfined freeze area Saturday night into the northeast quadrant).\nIn addition, the dry period and some gradient wind keep potential\nfire danger around today. Inherited HWO fire danger graphic \nremains on track.\n\nLate weekend into late next week (Sunday-Thursday): As shortwave \nridging builds in late weekend, a upper low will dive down out of \nthe Plains into the ArkLaTex region. This will bring broad \nisentropic ascent and moist advection into the region, but PWs \nonly climb to around an inch in the far southwest portion of the \narea. Majority of any rainfall observations will fall southwest of\nthe area, but any we see will likely be light sprinkles. There \nshould be more expectation of broad area of radar returns but more\n\"virga\" or evaporating rain rather than beneficial rainfall. Less\nthan a tenth is most likely but if more efficient moist advection\ncan occur, there could be a spot that may see up to that in \nnortheast LA and extreme southwest MS but this is likely very \ngenerous. After this shortwave moves through, dry easterly low- \nlevel flow will setup underneath a sprawling 1030mb surface high \nover the Appalachians to eastern seaboard. This will keep quiet\nweather and lead to gradual thermo moderation. A switch will flip \nfrom seasonably cool highs Monday (4F to 8F below into 58F to 62F\nrange) to seasonably warm around Wednesday to Thursday (4F to 8F \nabove into 68F to 78F range). Additional patchy frost or very \nisolated light freeze cannot be ruled out both Sunday and Monday \nnights, but this will be past the growing season of November 15 so\nno additional headlines or HWO graphics will be needed. /DC/",
